Work-in-progress version 0.2 of esxMON memory monitor and Z80 disassembler
for ZX Spectrum with ESXDOS 0.8.9 or better(?) supported devices. One of
the design(?) principles is to keep the code ROMmable i.e. no SMC, and no
variables outside stack or IX indexed 256 bytes "sysvar" area. However,
the 'r'egisters command somewhat broke this as some register values are
accessed directly to RAM, not via IX.. bummer..

Numbers are by default in hexdecimal. If they are preceded with _ in the
input line they are treated as decimal. 
Memory poke command also understands ASCII strings. A string is opened
with ' character and string ends to next white space of end of line.

Each command that takes <addr> as a parameter also changes the internal
memory pointer to <addr>. This implies the next 'm', 'mm', 'd' or 'dd'
command without <addr> uses that previously defined <addr>.

esxMON support simple line editing. You can delete characters, and move
left or right in the input line. Editing is always in 'insert' mode. The
input line is maximum 42 characters including the cursor.

Worth noting that 'r'egister command does not handle SP properly. When
'c'alling a subroutine the SP is loaded according the current SP the
esxMON uses itself. PC is completely ignored.

The .command syntax in BASIC is the following:
.esxmon [<file> <addr> [<off> [<len>]]


Supported commands:
  m [<addr>]                       : show 7 lines of 8 byte blocks of RAM
                                     starting from <addr>.
  mm [<addr>]                      : show 15 lines of 8 byte blocks of RAM
                                     starting from <addr>.
  p <addr> [<num>|'<str>] ..       : poke bytes(!) or strings separated by
                                   : white spaces into RAM starting from
								   : <addr>.
  b <bank>                         : set RAM page at $C000 to <bank>.
  ls [<dir>]                       : show content of <dir> or current dir.
  cd <dir>                         : change current working directory.
  l <file> <addr> [<off> [<len>]]  : load <file> into RAM <addr> optionally
                                   : at offset <off> (default 0) and optinally
								   : max <len> (default entire file)
  s <file> <addr> <len>            : save <file> from RAM <addr> and <len>
                                   : bytes.
  f <addr1> <addr2> <byte>         : fill memory between <addr1> and <addr2>
                                   : with value <byte>.
  d [<addr>]                       : Disassemble 7 opcodes starting from
                                   : <addr>.
  dd [<addr>]                      : Disassemble 15 opcodes starting from
                                   : <addr>.
  c <addr>                         : CALL subroutine at <addr>. Register 
                                   : visible with 'r' as passed to subroutine.
  r [<16-bit reg> <value>          : dump registers or set a register.
  x                                : exit to BASIC.
  h                                : print help page.


“We should forget about small efficiencies, say about 97% of the time:
premature optimization is the root of all evil. Yet we should not pass
up our opportunities in that critical 3%.”
   Donald Knuth, “Structured Programming with Go To Statements”,
                 ACM Computing Surveys, 1974. 

